5) O(n log n)

This is basically a combination of:

n

and:

log n

giving:

O(n log n)

You don't need to derive it yet.
You'll encounter it especially when we study sorting.
For now:
O(n log n) is generally much better than O(n²) for large inputs.
___________________________________________________________________________________
O(1)       → barely grows
O(log n)   → grows very slowly
O(n)       → grows steadily
O(n log n) → grows faster
O(n²)      → grows very quickly

shown in the image in other file 
A simple ranking

For large inputs, you can generally think:

             Faster
               ↑
O(1)           │
O(log n)       │
O(n)           │
O(n log n)     │
O(n²)          │
               ↓
             Slower

This isn't saying every O(n) program is literally faster than every O(log n) program in every situation. 
It's about asymptotic growth.
(Asymptotic growth rate is the long-term rate at which a model grows or declines as the input gets large.)
That distinction will matter later.
